# jotun guest manifest: the full description of a guest domain.
bundle bundle-depot
ref root
subtree /
init rc

# The guest's network name. Another co-running guest reaches this one's
# services by dialing "tcp!<name>!<port>"; the guest offers a service with
# net_listen("tcp!*!<port>"). An external host is dialed the same way, by its
# own name, which the host's name service resolves through DNS instead.
# name web

# A listen is in-host only unless a net rule names the port, in which case the
# same listen also binds a real external port. Offering a port externally is
# more sensitive than dialing out, so it is opt-in:
# net allow * 8080 tcp     # also bind 8080 for inbound connections

# Network policy: "net allow <who> <ports> [tcp|udp]". With no net rule the
# guest may reach anywhere; with one or more, only matching targets are allowed
# and everything else is refused. <who> is an IPv4 CIDR (matched against the
# resolved address of an external target), a co-running guest's name (an in-host
# link to that guest), or "*" for any target, external or in-host. ports is a
# single port, a "lo-hi" range, or "*"; the protocol defaults to both. The rules
# gate what a guest may reach, not what it may offer in-host.
# net allow 127.0.0.1/32 18080 tcp   # an external address
# net allow 10.0.0.0/8 1-1024
# net allow * 53 udp
# net allow web 8080 tcp             # may dial co-running guest "web" on 8080

# Graft a shared read-only tree at a top-level name in the guest root, in
# addition to the guest's own tree: "mount <name> <bundle> <ref> [subtree]".
# The ref may be a ref name, a pinned root hash, or a jimage, like bundle/ref
# above. The name must not already exist in the guest's own root.
# mount shared shared-depot root
